  "order": 13,
  "blocks": [
    {
      "id": "6g1hadn",
      "type": "heading",
      "level": 2,
      "content": "1. Introduction to Human Diseases"
    },
    {
      "type": "keyFacts",
      "title": "Key Takeaways",
      "items": [
        "Communicable diseases are caused by infectious pathogens like bacteria, viruses, fungi, and protozoa, whereas non-communicable diseases stem from genetic, nutritional, or lifestyle factors.",
        "Bacterial diseases such as Tuberculosis and Typhoid are treated with targeted antibiotics, while viral infections rely primarily on preventive vaccines and supportive care.",
        "Innate immunity offers immediate, non-specific defense mechanisms, while adaptive immunity delivers targeted responses with long-term immunological memory via T-cells and B-cells.",
        "Nutritional deficiency diseases arise from a lack of essential vitamins or minerals, such as Vitamin C deficiency causing Scurvy and Vitamin D deficiency leading to Rickets.",
        "Biological vectors like female Anopheles and Aedes aegypti mosquitoes act as primary transmitters for protozoan and viral diseases such as Malaria and Dengue."
      ],
      "id": "2jcwv6n"
    },
